==Dutch==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|nl|dum|pippe}}, {{m|dum|pip}}, {{m|dum|pips|t=pip”, also “cold, flu}}, ultimately from post-classical {{der|nl|la|pipita|pip(p)ita}}, from {{der|nl|la|pītuīta||slime, head cold}}.

The word was borrowed into West Germanic before the High German consonant shift as {{m|gmw-pro||*pippit}}, whence {{cog|goh|pfipfiz}} and ([[Central German]]) {{m|goh|pipz}}, {{m|goh||*pippiz}} (modern {{cog|de|Pips}}, obsolete {{m|de|Pfipfs}}). In Dutch and Low German we should expect a form such as {{m|dum||*pippet}}, which is not attested, however. One possibility is that these dialects borrowed the Central German form and the final ''s''-sound was later reanalysed as the genitive suffix. Middle Dutch also had {{m|dum|pipeye}}, from {{cog|fro|pipie}}.

